---
// src/pages/_site-updates/2026-08-24-invitation-only-access.astro
export const updateMeta = {
  date: '2026-08-24',
  title: 'Public pricing replaced by an invitation-only waitlist',
  tags: ['pricing', 'access', 'waitlist', 'seo'],
};
---
<article class="border-l-4 border-[hsl(24,95%,53%)] pl-6">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">August 24, 2026</h2>
  <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
    Public pricing is retired across the site. The three published tiers, the 14-day free trial,
    and the FOUNDER50 founding-member discount no longer appear anywhere on breederhq.com.
    Access to BreederHQ is invitation only: breeders apply through a waitlist and are onboarded
    one at a time with a founder-led setup.
  </p>

  <div class="mb-4">
    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Why</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">
      Onboarding capacity, not software licences, is what limits how many breeders can start at
      once. Every founding member receives a personal setup, so the site now asks for an
      application rather than a purchase. Publishing a price list alongside that would promise a
      self-serve path that does not exist.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="mb-4">
    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Replaced</h3>
    <ul class="text-sm text-gray-600 list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
      <li>
        <a href="/pricing" class="text-[hsl(24,95%,53%)] hover:underline">/pricing</a> is now a
        waitlist application: name, email, kennel or business name, species, and an optional
        phone or WhatsApp number. The URL is unchanged, so every existing inbound link still
        resolves. Submitting swaps the page for a confirmation screen rather than navigating away.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>/pricing/compare</code> is removed. Its three-column Standard / Pro / Enterprise
        matrix described products that can no longer be bought. It redirects to /pricing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/features" class="text-[hsl(24,95%,53%)] hover:underline">/features</a> keeps all
        132 capabilities but drops the tier filter, the per-card tier badges, and the plan-limits
        table. It reads as one capability list rather than a tier decision tool. Search still works.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="mb-4">
    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Site-wide</h3>
    <ul class="text-sm text-gray-600 list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
      <li>The header banner now reads "Invitation only" and points at the waitlist.</li>
      <li>
        Every breeder call to action across roughly 90 pages now targets /pricing instead of
        self-serve registration. Buyer and service-provider signup are unaffected: both remain
        open, and their "free account" copy is accurate.
      </li>
      <li>
        Comparison, species, and landing pages had their pricing and discount arguments rewritten
        around limited access and founder-led setup. Competitor pricing and competitor trial
        lengths are factual research and were left exactly as they were.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="mb-4">
    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Machine-readable surfaces</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">
      <code>/pricing.md</code>, <code>/okf/pricing.md</code>, <code>/okf/product.md</code>,
      <code>/okf/glossary.md</code> and <code>/llms.txt</code> were rewritten to state that access
      is invitation only. These files are how AI assistants answer "how much does BreederHQ cost",
      so leaving them stale would have kept a retired price list in circulation regardless of what
      the site itself said. They each explicitly note that the trial is retired, which stops an
      older cached answer being repeated as current. The marketplace listing-fee schedule moved
      here from the old pricing page: still answerable, no longer competing with the application.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Retired</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">
      <code>/founders</code> (the FOUNDER50 offer page) and <code>/dog-show-signup</code> with its
      booth loop video and manifests are removed; both redirect to /pricing so printed QR codes and
      existing links do not 404. <code>/coming-soon</code> now points straight at /pricing rather
      than chaining through /founders.
    </p>
  </div>
</article>
